<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary Wollstonecraft</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary Wollstonecraft was an English writer and philosopher, regarded as one of the founding feminist philosophers. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, she won fame with A Vindication of the Rights of Men (1790), a reply to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>'s critique of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-vindication-of-the-rights-of-woman">A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</a> (1792) she argued that women only appeared inferior to men because they lacked education, and that both should be treated as rational beings. Her widower, the philosopher William Godwin, published a memoir of her life in 1798 that damaged her reputation for almost a century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft.jpg" alt="Mary Wollstonecraft" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Opie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mary_Wollstonecraft_by_John_Opie_(c._1797).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many writers called for useful knowledge, wider education and the freedom of the press, and some, like the Marquis de Condorcet and <b>Mary Wollstonecraft</b>, extended the argument for equal rights to women.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Mary Shelley, the daughter of the philosopher <b>Mary Wollstonecraft</b>, wrote in Frankenstein one of the most enduring books of the age.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In England <b>Mary Wollstonecraft</b>’s A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) argued that women were rational beings who deserved the same education as men.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mary Wollstonecraft</b> is seen by many as its founder because of her 1792 book A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, in which she argues that class and private property are the basis of discrimination against women and that women as much as men need equal rights.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mary Wollstonecraft</b>’s A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) founded liberal feminism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This is the very point I aim at. I do not wish them to have power over men; but over themselves.&#8221;</p><footer>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), chapter 4 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3420/pg3420-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is time to effect a revolution in female manners, time to restore to them their lost dignity, and make them, as a part of the human species, labour by reforming themselves to reform the world.&#8221;</p><footer>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), chapter 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3420/pg3420-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Strengthen the female mind by enlarging it, and there will be an end to blind obedience…&#8221;</p><footer>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), chapter 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3420/pg3420-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Taught from their infancy, that beauty is woman's sceptre, the mind shapes itself to the body, and, roaming round its gilt cage, only seeks to adorn its prison.&#8221;</p><footer>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), chapter 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3420/pg3420-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But, if women are to be excluded, without having a voice, from a participation of the natural rights of mankind, prove first, to ward off the charge of injustice and inconsistency, that they want reason…&#8221;</p><footer>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), dedication to Talleyrand &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3420/pg3420-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
